hey didn't develop from  tuscan but developed as sister dialects  to tusken there are around 3 million  speakers of these dialects  keep in mind that the number of speakers  for these regional languages might be  misleading because virtually everybody  is also fluent in standard italian  and their use of the regional languages  might be limited to certain situations  for example in communicating with their  elderly relatives  in smaller numbers there are also the  languages spoken in neighboring  countries either as regional or national  languages  german there are about 293 000 speakers  in northern areas of italy adjacent to  germany austria and switzerland  franco provencal has around 90 000  speakers  in the north near france mostly in  piedmont albanian  has around 80 000 speakers slovene has  around 70 000 speakers  near the border with slovenia oxyton has  around 40 000 speakers in piedmont and  liguria  catalan is spoken by around 20 000  people in the city of  algero on sardinia which began as a  catalan settlement  keeping in mind that the regional  romance languages are generally  considered dialects in italy i thought  it would be fun to  have a look at some of them and see how  similar or different they are let's look  at standard italian neapolitan sardinian  and friulian  vodka this means i would like something  to read  word for word it's would like first  person singular  something to read and a neapolitan  here the words all correspond and if  you're looking for it you can see the  connection between the words  but the words are also different enough  to make comprehension difficult  neapolitan words are often shorter than  the italian equivalent with final vowels  or  final syllables reduced in italian  there's vorae  this becomes vules in neapolitan i've  also seen this spelled with an e  at the end neapolitan spelling is not  standardized  italian qualicosa in neapolitan  the italian preposition da meaning from  or by  but also used before an in