r  here we see that the object pronoun m  contracts with the verb  notice again that in neapolitan the  final vowel becomes reduced to a schwa  sound the main verb here is the  equivalent of  deto in italian d sounds in italian  often correspond to r  sounds in neapolitan the word k is  pronounced k  when unstressed and contracts with the  following verb  because of its initial vowel sound the  italian word arivaremo  is arivam in neapolitan pe is the  equivalent of  per in italian meaning for  becomes once again notice all those  reduced sounds  in sardinia  here we can see that similar to the  other languages in sardinian the past  can be expressed by using the auxiliary  verb meaning to have  even though it looks quite different the  un sound is a hint  the main verb however is different i'll  speculate that this verb now  is related to the latin verb narat  meaning t'tel  or narrate though i'm not sure k in  italian  is ka the future tense is formed  differently in sardinia with an  auxiliary verb  here instead of veraso we see akantade  which is similar to the italian  preposition akantoa  which means beside here we have a  different definite article  and undici becomes undigi in friulian  amiendita que rivarinto in friulian it  seems that rather than use a specific  indirect object pronoun  the preposition a is used with the  object pronoun  like to me here andite  is the past tense formed the same way as  in italian and neapolitan but with  different forms  here we have the subject pronoun which  is not necessary in the other languages  and here we have the verb for arrive but  you might not have immediately  recognized it because it begins with a  single  r rather than an a and a double r tor  is the preposition meaning around and  undis  this sentence means i have two brothers  and three sisters  word for word it's have first person  singular present tense  two brothers and three sisters in  neapolitan  in the italian sentence notice that the  verb for half is  o a form of the verb avere but in the  neapolitan sentence  it's teng this is similar to the word  used in spanish  in italian there's a word like this  tenere which would be tengo  in the first person singular but that  means to hold like in your hand  not to have in the general sense here  the word for two is slightly different  here the word for brothers is fratta  rather than fratelli and the word for  sisters is also shortened so  rather than sorely and notice that the  reduction of the endings make the gender  of nouns somewhat unclear  in this case of course we know because  of their meaning but with other nouns  the gender might be less clear  in sardinia  here the verb tenju is similar to the  neapolitan one  tenga two is deus rather than due  and three is tres rather than trey both  the verb  and the numbers remind me of spanish  here next  fraris and soris mean brother and sister  respectively  and notice that they form the plural by  adding an s this is how it's done in  many romance languages  but not in standard italian in which the  final vowel of the word indicates number  infrijulian  again we see that the pronoun can't be  dropped in freulean  here we see the verb to have which is a  cognate with the verb in the italian  sentence  unlike the ones in the neapolitan and  sardinian sentences  it's pronounced i but you can see a  connection with the french word a  tu is dos like in spanish the word for  brother is basically the same as the  sardinian word  and the plural is formed by adding s the  word for sister  sor is like the french word sur  as you can see from these examples  there's a lot of linguistic diversity in  italy with regional languages and their  local dialects  having their own colorful features that  reflect the history and the traditions  of their speakers  many of these languages and dialects are  endangered and risk being completely  replaced by standard italian in the  coming generations  there are some efforts to preserve t